## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.ts** (`ContentstackContext`): This is the single React context that feeds all Contentstack content into the component tree (via useContent → getValue). Every component reads from it. Live Preview requires the context to carry preview content and update reactively — currently it is a static shape with no preview/draft fields, no live update mechanism, and no connection to the Contentstack Live Preview SDK. Without extending this context (and its provider) to accept and reactively update preview data, no downstream component can ever render live-preview content.
  - **Minimal fix:** Extend IContentstackContext to include a live_preview object (Contentstack SDK provides this shape: { live_preview: string, content_type_uid: string, entry_uid: string }) and a mechanism to merge preview data into the context. The provider that wraps this context (likely in _app.tsx or a layout) must initialize the Contentstack Live Preview SDK and subscribe to its real-time updates, merging preview field values into the existing content object so useContent/getValue returns the preview value instead of the published one. The Contentstack JavaScript SDK's livePreview method (from 'contentstack') should be used to register the preview listener — reuse the existing Contentstack SDK instance if one is already initialized in the project's data-fetching layer.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given the site is opened via the CMS's Live Preview entry point, the tester observes the page render with the published content displayed normally, with no blank areas, errors, or missing sections.
- When a content editor changes a field in the CMS and saves it, the tester observes the corresponding text or image on the previewed page update to the new value without manually reloading the browser.
- When a content editor saves draft or unpublished changes in the CMS, the tester observes those changes visible on the previewed page, while the same URL viewed as a normal visitor continues to display the previously published content.
- When the connection between the previewed page and the CMS is interrupted, the tester observes the page either display a visible indication that live updates are unavailable or continue showing the last published content, without crashing, going blank, or silently presenting unsaved edits as live content.
- When the site is visited normally outside the CMS preview experience, the tester observes only published content rendered and no on-screen updates occurring in response to CMS edits.
- After a content edit is saved in the CMS, the tester observes the updated value reflected everywhere that content appears on the previewed site, with no page or section continuing to display the old value.

